  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nMain forecast concern today continues to be another round of\nshowers and storms as a shortwave trough, embedded in west-\nnorthwest flow aloft, approaches during the late morning and \nafternoon hours. This feature, combined with a northward \nlifting warm front, in a weak to moderately unstable airmass, \nwill set the stage for another round of storms, some of which \nmay become severe. As a result, SPC has maintained a Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 of 5) for areas along and south of Interstate 90.\n\nAvailable high-res models to include both the HRRR and 3km NAM,\nshow developing storms after the 18z timeframe. Fcst soundings\nfrom across the area show steep low-level lapse rates thanks to\ntemperatures warming into the lower to middle 80s later today.\nThis combined with increasing moisture advection behind the\nwarm front, should support MLCAPE values from 1000-1500 J/kg \nacross the area later today. Despite decent synoptic forcing \nwith the approaching shortwave, main limiting factor for a more \nwidespread severe threat appears to be weaker deep layered shear\nfields as 0-6km bulk shear values are expected to range from \n30-35 kts eastern NY and western New England, while stronger  \nshear values remain out west across the southern Ontario and \nwestern New York. \n\nGiven decent dry air aloft along with steep low-level lapse\nrates, the main threat with any storms that approach severe \nlimits will be damaging wind gusts. That said, a humid airmass\nwill once again lead to efficient rainfall with any storms, and\nWPC has also maintained a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for the \nentire fcst area. Storms should track east and gradually weaken\nafter the 00z timeframe with loss of daytime heating. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe next chance for rain will be Wednesday night into Thursday\nmorning as another shortwave trough and associated cold front \napproach the region.  As of right now, precipitation looks to \nmove in after the 00z Thursday time frame. Given the time of \nday, instability looks to remain fairly limited, and thunder \nactivity should be isolated in nature.",
